## Beacon helps coding agents reuse lessons after human review

Source: Asymptote Labs — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50182883
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: hn

Beacon is an open-source project for carrying lessons between coding agents. Switching tools can mean explaining the same workflow or past mistake again. In an October 21 announcement, its creators describe recording prompts, tool calls and commands across more than twenty agent tools, then converting those records into reusable knowledge.

The important step is human review. Beacon identifies recurring patterns and workflows, asks for approval, and makes reviewed lessons available to future agents. For illustration, a team could review a lesson about its repository’s test workflow before making that guidance available to another coding agent. This is an example of intended use, not a measured outcome.

Technically, session records share a common format. Approved knowledge is exposed through skills containing reusable instructions, and the Model Context Protocol (MCP), which connects agents to external tools and data. The announcement does not independently establish that capture is complete or that reused lessons improve results.

Why it matters: For teams switching coding agents: check the original session evidence before approving a lesson for reuse.

