## How Langfuse limits Halo’s access during background investigations

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/blog/2026-10-21-building-the-langfuse-assistant
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: news

Langfuse helps engineers inspect AI application runs. Its Halo assistant can investigate those records and help with dashboards or evaluations. An October 21 engineering post explains how this existing assistant works, including how it keeps running after you close the browser.

For illustration, you could ask Halo to group errors across failed runs, close the tab, and return to saved progress. Background workers perform the investigation independently of the browser. Access still follows your project permissions, and actions that change data require approval.

The implementation separates data access from script execution. A temporary project key and role-filtered tools control access through the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an interface for agent tools. An optional isolated script environment has no network access by default and receives only authorized data. Postgres stores progress; BullMQ queues work. Failed runs are not automatically retried, so background execution alone does not guarantee recovery. This is an architecture account, not a security certification.

Why it matters: For engineers building investigative assistants: test project permissions, approval steps and failure recovery separately.

