## Braintrust adds application logs to help explain agent runs

Source: Braintrust — https://github.com/braintrustdata/braintrust-sdk-javascript/releases/tag/braintrust%403.39.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Braintrust records what happens during an AI application run so engineers can investigate failures. Its JavaScript SDK, the library added to an application, now supports application logging and console capture. Version 3.39.0 adds this context alongside traces, records of individual steps such as model calls, and fixes some incorrect usage records.

For illustration, an assistant might log that it used a fallback before calling a model. That application message could help explain an unexpected response alongside the recorded model call. Engineers should check what their integration actually captures; the announcement does not promise automatic coverage of every application path.

Technical changes include AI SDK batch and code-mode tracing. The release fixes streamed tool calls being incorrectly combined when certain providers omit an index. It adds text and usage recording for streamed Bedrock InvokeModel calls to OpenAI models, and includes cached input reads and writes in prompt_tokens for Pi and Flue integrations. Those accounting changes can affect comparisons with older runs. The notes provide no independent benchmark for logging overhead or cost accuracy.

Why it matters: For JavaScript agent teams: inspect captured logs, streamed tool calls and token totals after upgrading.

