## LangWatch compresses trace uploads and fixes how records are read

Source: LangWatch — https://github.com/langwatch/langwatch/releases/tag/langwatch%40v3.21.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

LangWatch collects traces, records of the steps an AI application takes, for debugging and analysis. Version 3.21.0 changes how those records travel from an application to LangWatch. Its SDKs now send compressed trace data by default, and the server can recognize compression even when a request’s format label is wrong.

For illustration, a team upgrading behind a proxy should send a known trace through that same route and check that it arrives with the expected usage and tool output. A successful upload alone does not prove that every field was interpreted correctly. The release includes fixes for reading Claude traces, including counting usage once and preserving large attributes and tool output.

The transport uses gzip-compressed OTLP/protobuf, OpenTelemetry’s binary telemetry format. The server detects gzip or zstd from identifying bytes in the data. Other changes improve queue labels and age metrics, and allow retrying or discarding more than 2,000 groups of failed work at once. No measured bandwidth reduction is supplied.

Why it matters: For LangWatch operators: verify trace contents through your proxy and check visibility into queued or failed work.

