## Phoenix sends uncaught browser errors to the server log

Source: Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/arize-phoenix-v20.24.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Phoenix is an interface for inspecting AI traces and evaluation results. If that interface breaks in a user’s browser, application traces alone may not explain the problem. Version 20.24.0 adds reporting of uncaught frontend errors to the server log, giving operators another place to investigate.

For illustration, a team testing the upgrade could trigger a controlled browser error and check whether its details reach the deployed server’s logs. The release notes do not describe automatic alerts or demonstrate that every browser failure will be detected. Error reporting still needs a check in the environment users actually access.

The same release excludes LiteLLM 1.107.0, raises the certifi minimum to 2026.10.18, and requires Node releases containing the October security patches. These are requirements for supporting packages and the runtime. Installing a new Phoenix version does not by itself confirm which versions remain in a lockfile, container image or running process. Check the resolved deployment as well as the package version.

Why it matters: For Phoenix operators: exercise browser-error reporting and verify the dependency versions actually deployed.

