## Phoenix changes how AI evaluators return labels and refusals

Source: Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/%40arizeai/phoenix-evals%402.7.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Phoenix Evals helps engineers use AI models to judge outputs, for example by assigning a quality label. Version 2.7.0 adds support for decision models through the AI SDK library. It also changes how existing evaluation models receive prompts and report results, so old and new scores need careful comparison.

Consider an illustrative check that labels an answer as passing or failing. If the judging model refuses to decide, the application must handle an error instead of receiving an ordinary label. Counting that refusal as a pass would misrepresent the evaluation. The release itself does not claim better judging accuracy.

Under the hood, experimental_decide returns labels, with probabilities and provider confidence recorded when available. Prompts become plain text; refusals throw Experimental_DecisionRefusalError. Decision spans, records of individual decisions, capture state, question and answer. Because this interface is experimental, the release pins ai to 7.0.137 and @ai-sdk/otel to 1.0.137. OpenAI decision models require @ai-sdk/openai 4.0.89 or later.

Why it matters: For evaluation owners: test prompts and refusals before comparing upgraded runs with historical scores.

